Vision Impairment (Cataracts/Macular Degeneration/Glaucoma)
Geriatric & Frailty
Gradual painless blurred vision, Increased glare sensitivity/poor night vision, Central or peripheral visual field loss, Opacified lens/drusen/disc cupping

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-050
Reason for Visit: Acute panic attack, disorientation, and a minor fall in the home during a power outage, exacerbated by profound visual deficits.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and neighbor
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a frail patient with known severe visual impairment who experienced a minor fall and a severe panic attack when a localized power outage plunged their home into darkness, eliminating their remaining functional vision. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to rule out occult fractures or head trauma from the fall, assess for acute neurological events, and provide immediate psychological de-escalation and environmental safety modifications prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The lights went out, I couldn't see my own hand in front of my face, I tripped over the rug, and I was terrified I was having a stroke."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a long-standing history of complex vision impairment. They describe gradual, painless blurring of vision and increased glare sensitivity indicating progressive cataracts. Concurrently, they report a history of insidious, painless loss of peripheral vision resulting in tunnel vision, indicating chronic open-angle glaucoma. (They do not report central visual field loss or severe distortion of straight lines to suggest macular degeneration). They note a frequent need for prescription changes in corrective eyewear with minimal improvement. They have severe difficulty driving at night due to poor night vision. Tonight, during a brief neighborhood power outage, their reliance on bright ambient light was completely compromised. This sudden total sensory deprivation caused extreme disorientation, leading to a minor fall in the hallway and a severe panic attack. They deny any loss of consciousness, severe head trauma, or acute chest pain.
Functional Status: Usually independent in a highly adapted, brightly lit home environment; acutely distressed and fearful of moving.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent, injurious falls in low-light environments; severe anxiety regarding impending total blindness.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/90 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 71 Kg
General: Highly anxious, sitting rigidly on the sofa, clutching the neighbor's arm, but alert and oriented.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal and neurological exams reveal no signs of blunt force trauma, focal neurological deficits, or fractures from the minor fall. The patient has full range of motion in all limbs and no bony point tenderness. Cranial nerve examination confirms grossly intact extraocular movements. Ophthalmological evaluation using a penlight reveals a visible cloudy opacification of both lenses (cataracts). Confrontation visual field testing demonstrates profound peripheral visual field loss bilaterally (tunnel vision), consistent with their known glaucoma. The pupils are sluggish but reactive. (Elevated intraocular pressure or optic disc cupping on specialized ophthalmic exam are anticipated diagnostic tetrad findings from their specialist records).
Environmental Observations: The power has been restored, but the hallway where the fall occurred is noted to have a loose rug and poor contrast between the walls and the floor.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient is compliant with daily intraocular pressure-lowering eye drops (e.g., latanoprost).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Vision Impairment (Cataracts and Glaucoma) resulting in an acute environment-induced panic and fall - Status: Acute Exacerbation of Frailty
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke (Occipital Lobe) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The vision loss is chronic and bilateral, with a clear mechanical/environmental trigger (the power outage) for the sudden disorientation. There are no new acute focal motor or sensory deficits.

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of acute, severe, unilateral ocular pain, headache, nausea, vomiting, or a fixed, mid-dilated pupil. The patient's glaucoma is the chronic, open-angle variant.

Retinal Detachment - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of a sudden shower of new floaters, flashes of light, or a specific "curtain" falling over one eye.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Risk of traumatic falls in poor lighting)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient experienced a severe psychological and mechanical crisis (panic attack and fall) directly caused by the sudden failure of their highly adapted visual environment (the power outage), which unmasked the true severity of their chronic cataracts and glaucoma. Telephone triage correctly prioritized the need to rule out physical trauma from the fall and acute neurological pathology. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have left the patient in a state of terror, convinced they had suffered a stroke, and highly vulnerable to further falls if they attempted to mobilize in the dark again. This urgent after-hours intervention allowed for the safe, physical exclusion of acute fractures or stroke, provided immediate reassurance that the event was environmentally triggered, and allowed for the implementation of robust, fail-safe lighting strategies to prevent recurrence.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted a thorough physical assessment to reassure the patient they had not suffered a stroke or broken any bones in the fall. Re-established the patient's orientation to their environment once the lights were back on. Ensured compliance with their regular glaucoma eye drops.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a "torch and pause" emergency lighting routine. Step 1: The patient places heavy-duty, automatically activating emergency plug-in flashlights in every hallway and the bathroom. Additionally, they must pause and stand perfectly still for 10 seconds whenever moving from a bright room to a dark room to allow their retinas time to adjust. Step 2: Upon successfully utilizing this pause technique safely, the patient rewards themselves with a brief, calming sensory activity, such as listening to a preferred radio station. Cause-and-effect: The automatic lights completely remove the environmental trigger (darkness) during outages. The deliberate 10-second pause mechanically prevents the patient from walking into obstacles while "blind," and the reward reduces the anxiety associated with the slow transition, restoring their confidence in moving safely around their home.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Initiated a referral to an occupational therapist for a comprehensive low-vision home safety assessment.
DME/Safety (Optional): Urgently recommended the removal of the hallway rug and the immediate purchase of plug-in, battery-backed emergency lights.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 1-2 weeks to arrange a formal ophthalmology review regarding potential cataract extraction to maximize remaining vision.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department if the patient experiences a sudden, severe pain in the eye, completely loses all vision suddenly, or suffers a physical fall resulting in head trauma or an inability to bear weight.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The physical exam shows no broken bones or signs of a stroke tonight; the disorientation was a direct result of your severe visual impairment combining with the dark environment.
Clinical Course: Your vision will not suddenly improve on its own. You must rely on maximum lighting and environmental safety changes to prevent future falls. An eye specialist can determine if removing the cataracts will help your usable vision.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if you fall and hit your head, if you develop sudden, intense pain in your eye, or if your vision goes completely black. You must see your GP next week to discuss seeing your eye specialist again.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You did not have a stroke. Because of your cataracts (cloudy lenses) and glaucoma (loss of side vision), you require incredibly bright light to see anything at all. When the power went out, your brain lost all its visual input instantly, causing a massive, terrifying disorientation and the fall. We need to create a fail-safe environment so this never happens again.
Actionable Steps: Remove the loose rug in the hallway immediately. Have your neighbor purchase automatic emergency plug-in lights tomorrow. Always pause for 10 seconds when walking into a darker room to let your eyes adjust.

